    <div class="item"><div class="car" data-title="Daily AI brief" tabindex="0" aria-label="Daily AI brief photos - press Enter to enlarge"><div class="car-track"><img src="/work/ai-arch.jpg" alt="System architecture diagram of the AI news brief pipeline" loading="lazy" style="object-fit:contain;background:var(--bg-2)" /><img src="/work/ai2.jpg" alt="Daily AI news brief email in the inbox" loading="lazy" style="object-fit:contain;background:var(--bg-2)" /></div><button class="car-prev" type="button" aria-label="Previous photo">&#8249;</button><button class="car-next" type="button" aria-label="Next photo">&#8250;</button><span class="car-count"></span></div><div>
      <p class="tag">Software</p><h3>Daily AI brief</h3>
      <p>A fully automated AI news brief that lands in my inbox at 7am every morning without me lifting a finger. A Claude Code routine runs in the cloud on a cron, researches the last day or two of AI news with live web search, writes a <a href="/work/ai-brief-sample.html">5-7 minute read</a> organized around my interests, compares and deduplicates it against the previous days' briefs so nothing repeats, then commits and pushes the file to a GitHub repo.</p>
      <details><summary>More</summary><p>But the routine's sandbox is network-locked and can't actually send mail, so the git push trips a GitHub Action running on open network that renders the markdown to HTML and sends it through Resend's API to my email. All the editorial rules live in a CLAUDE.md that the routine reads fresh on every run, so tweaking what it covers, how it writes, and who it addresses is just a matter of editing that one file.</p><p>In fact, if you'd like to sign up for it and stay up to date on the latest in AI, click the button below :)</p></details>
      <a class="cta" href="/work/ai-brief-signup.html">Sign up</a></div></div>

    <div class="item"><div class="car" data-title="CragPrints" tabindex="0" aria-label="CragPrints photos - press Enter to enlarge"><div class="car-track"><img src="/work/cragprints-cover.jpg" alt="CragPrints logo and wordmark beside a tee printed with El Capitan" loading="lazy" style="object-fit:contain;background:var(--bg-2)" /><img src="/work/cragprints.jpg" alt="Natural CragPrints tee with a line drawing of El Capitan on the back" loading="lazy" style="object-fit:contain;background:var(--bg-2)" /><img src="/work/cragprints-3.jpg" alt="Black CragPrints tee with a light line drawing of Half Dome on the back" loading="lazy" style="object-fit:contain;background:var(--bg-2)" /></div><button class="car-prev" type="button" aria-label="Previous photo">&#8249;</button><button class="car-next" type="button" aria-label="Next photo">&#8250;</button><span class="car-count"></span></div><div>
      <p class="tag">Software</p><h3>CragPrints</h3>
      <p>My solo climbing-apparel venture. Climbers have great appreciation for the rock formations their routes and problems are found on. CragPrints lets them wear their sends and honor the natural places where they find adventure. Every design is a detailed line drawing of a real rock formation - its cracks, ledges, and holds - printed on the back of a shirt, with the climb's coordinates on the front as a subtle nod to where it can be found.</p>
      <details><summary>More</summary><p>There are two lines: Our Climbs, a curated collection of renowned boulders, routes, big walls, and peaks, and Your Send, where you upload a photo of your own project or objective and get it drawn in the same style, with multiple versions to choose from and fine-tune.</p><p>The whole store is one Astro app running on a Cloudflare Worker, and the store itself is the design builder. Our Climbs designs come out of a Python pipeline that turns reference photos into line art with an AI image model, then lays out print-ready files for each garment. Your Send runs the same idea live: the customer's photo is drawn by AI, and their own browser renders the print file, so the preview is exactly what gets printed.</p><p>Nothing is pre-made. At checkout, the product is created on the fly through Fourthwall's API, and Fourthwall handles payment, printing, and shipping. A small admin dashboard tracks orders and sales by channel, and lets me publish customers' designs into the collection.</p></details>
      <a class="cta" href="https://cragprints.com/dg">Visit CragPrints</a></div></div>

